F17 (found live, two consecutive fresh-critic rejections): the critic evidence diff is taken against the project's creation-era HEAD, so it accumulates EVERY uncheckpointed round's changes, while the builder's summary covers only the current round. The fresh critic therefore rejects hand-overs for "summary/diff mismatch" and "scope violation" that are really attribution artifacts — it cannot tell which changes belong to the round under review. (The pass-start repo-status baseline already solves this for the repo level; the project's own git has no equivalent.)

Fix: in POST /api/passes/run, at pass start — after the existing .heart-git init branch and the .gitignore hygiene, and BEFORE the scope baselines are captured and the builder is dispatched — if the project's worktree is dirty (gitInProject status --porcelain non-empty), stage everything and commit a snapshot with message `snapshot: pass baseline` (same git identity flags as the other program commits: -c user.name=Heart Studio -c user.email=heart@localhost), and log it in the pass job and the pass state history (event 'baseline', detail 'snapshot: pass baseline'). If the worktree is clean, do nothing. If the snapshot commit fails, fail the pass start with the git error — do not dispatch a builder on a broken baseline. This makes the round's diff-vs-HEAD exactly the round's own changes, which is what the critics judge. Do not change anything else in the flow.

